{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-128235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the family of functions $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+k},$ where $k$ is a constant.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=x-2$ and $v(x)={x}^{2}+4x+k,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =2x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)({x}^{2}+4x+k)-(x-2)(2x+4)}{{({x}^{2}+4x+k)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4x+k)-(2{x}^{2}-8)}{{({x}^{2}+4x+k)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x+k+8}{{({x}^{2}+4x+k)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "412ff4f1d566c1c5e5388c9e54c04445346b0d3206fe88c5dae98247dd48256e"} {"question" "q-128235" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128235" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{-x^2+4x+k+8}{(x^2+4x+k)^2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the family of functions $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+k},$ where $k$ is a constant.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-x^2+4x+k+8}{(x^2+4x+k)^2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the value of $k$ such that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is equal to $0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $k\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x+k+8}{{({x}^{2}+4x+k)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{-{0}^{2}+4\\cdot 0+k+8}{{({0}^{2}+4\\cdot 0+k)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{k+8}{{k}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve when $x=0$ equals $\\frac{k+8}{{k}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, using the fact that  the slope must be equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+8}{{k}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ k+8 & =0 \\\\ k & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "412ff4f1d566c1c5e5388c9e54c04445346b0d3206fe88c5dae98247dd48256e"} {"question" "q-128236" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128236" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the family of functions $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+k},$ where $k$ is a constant.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-x^2+4x+k+8}{(x^2+4x+k)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the value of $k$ such that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is equal to $0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $k\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhen $k=3,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "When $k=3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}=\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above expression through by $(x+3)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\nx-2=A(x+1)+B(x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3$ gets rid of the $B$ term and allows us to solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3-2 & =A(-3+1)+B(-3+3) \\\\ -5 & =-2A \\\\ A & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1$ gets rid of the $A$ term and allows us to solve for $B:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1-2 & =A(-1+1)+B(-1+3) \\\\ -3 & =2B \\\\ B & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "412ff4f1d566c1c5e5388c9e54c04445346b0d3206fe88c5dae98247dd48256e"} {"question" "q-128237" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128237" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128237" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128237" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the family of functions $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+k},$ where $k$ is a constant.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-x^2+4x+k+8}{(x^2+4x+k)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the value of $k$ such that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is equal to $0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $k\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWhen $k=3,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can integrate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3{|}^{5}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1{|}^{3})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x+3)^{5}}{(x+1)^{3}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x+3)^{5}}{(x+1)^{3}}|]}_{-5}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{4}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{4}^{3}}{{2}^{5}\\cdot {3}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "412ff4f1d566c1c5e5388c9e54c04445346b0d3206fe88c5dae98247dd48256e"} {"question" "q-128238" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128238" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(2)-\\frac{3}{2}\\ln(3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the family of functions $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+k},$ where $k$ is a constant.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-x^2+4x+k+8}{(x^2+4x+k)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the value of $k$ such that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is equal to $0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $k\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWhen $k=3,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFind the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(2)-\\frac{3}{2}\\ln(3)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhen $k=4,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "When $k=4,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+4}=\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =A(x+2)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to solve for $A$ and $B,$ and both of these constants appear in the equation simultaneously. However, we can eliminate $A$ if we substitute $x=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2-2 & =A(-2+2)+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve for $A.$ To do this, let’s start by substituting our value $B=-4$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =A(x+2)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute some value for $x$ and solve for $A.$\n\nWe *can’t* choose $x=-2,$ since this would eliminate $A$ from the equation. But we can choose any other value of $x.$ So let’s choose a simple value, say, $x=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-2 & =A(0+2)-4 \\\\ -2 & =2A-4 \\\\ 2 & =2A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "412ff4f1d566c1c5e5388c9e54c04445346b0d3206fe88c5dae98247dd48256e"} {"question" "q-128239" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128239" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128239" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128239" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the family of functions $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+k},$ where $k$ is a constant.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-x^2+4x+k+8}{(x^2+4x+k)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the value of $k$ such that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is equal to $0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $k\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nWhen $k=3,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFind the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(2)-\\frac{3}{2}\\ln(3)$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nWhen $k=4,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ in the case when $k=4.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Earlier, we found that when $k=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{4}{x+2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+1]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+2] \\\\  & =[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+2] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "412ff4f1d566c1c5e5388c9e54c04445346b0d3206fe88c5dae98247dd48256e"} {"question" "q-128240" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"} {"question" "q-128240" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ln(2)-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055103/state.json" "file_sha256" "6dd2821488c0fcc1076f844f278663d63409b0a7710966c77e15ba980ab95b1e"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1816 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
